        "question": {
          ":question/id": "77db7d37-419e-5690-b35c-822be3495e6f",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=- 2 y\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=4 e^{- 2 x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 8 e^{- 2 x}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=4 e^{- 2 x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(4 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=4\\cdot (-2)\\,e^{- 2 x} \\\\ &=- 8 e^{- 2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4 e^{- 2 x}$ and $y\\prime=- 8 e^{- 2 x}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}- 2 y \\\\ - 8 e^{- 2 x} &\\overset{?}{=}-2\\left(4 e^{- 2 x}\\right) \\\\ - 8 e^{- 2 x} &=- 8 e^{- 2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4 e^{- 2 x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
