        "question": {
          ":question/id": "184b4ab7-bae1-541d-afcf-a0d56e5393cc",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=- 4 e^{\\frac{y}{2}},\\qquad 2 x + 4>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 2 \\ln{\\left(2 x + 4 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- \\frac{4}{2 x + 4}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=- 2 \\ln{\\left(2 x + 4 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 2 \\ln{\\left(2 x + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-2\\cdot\\frac{2}{2 x + 4} \\\\ &=- \\frac{4}{2 x + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(2 x + 4 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=- \\frac{4}{2 x + 4}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}- 4 e^{\\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{2 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}-4e^{-(- 2 \\ln{\\left(2 x + 4 \\right)})/(-2)} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{2 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}-4e^{-\\ln(2 x + 4)} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{2 x + 4} &=- \\frac{2}{x + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(2 x + 4 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
